WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 3.9 · Structure, Governance & Accountability
Coordination and internal communication

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Reliable hand-offs need a named recipient, an agreed output and a date, even between two co-founders.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:23.319
Coordination makes work move reliably between people. It rests on clear hand-offs, a regular operating rhythm and agreed channels for communication and decisions.

4
00:00:23.719 --> 00:00:24.319
Three building blocks

5
00:00:24.319 --> 00:00:37.130
Clear hand-offs: A named recipient, an agreed output and a date. Sharing a file is not enough. For example: The baker hands the sales founder a price list by Friday for the market stall.

6
00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:49.941
Operating rhythm: A weekly check-in, a monthly look at numbers, a quarterly review. Add more as you grow. For example: The repair café founders meet every Monday for twenty minutes on priorities and blockers.

7
00:00:49.941 --> 00:01:04.322
Agreed channels: What goes in chat, what goes by email, where documents live and where decisions are recorded. For example: The youth club keeps quick updates in chat and every decision in one shared log.

8
00:01:04.722 --> 00:01:05.322
Why it matters

9
00:01:05.322 --> 00:01:10.996
1. Work falls through the gaps when nobody agreed who acts next.

10
00:01:10.996 --> 00:01:15.559
2. Meetings stay short when updates are shared beforehand.

11
00:01:15.559 --> 00:01:22.974
3. A decision log shows people who join later what was decided and why.

12
00:01:23.374 --> 00:01:23.974
What it guides

13
00:01:23.974 --> 00:01:28.907
1. Your planning calendar and weekly routines in Module 5.

14
00:01:28.907 --> 00:01:34.952
2. Your action tracking: an owner and a date for each meeting action.

15
00:01:34.952 --> 00:01:41.626
3. Your workload check: a route for flagging overload and clashing priorities.

16
00:01:42.026 --> 00:01:42.626
What to avoid

17
00:01:42.626 --> 00:01:48.300
Assuming that sharing a document means someone will act on it.

18
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.863
Using meetings for routine updates instead of decisions.

19
00:01:52.863 --> 00:01:59.537
Ignoring founder overload because there is no way to flag it.

20
00:02:00.137 --> 00:02:01.937
You define your operating rhythm, channels and one critical hand-off.

21
00:02:01.937 --> 00:02:05.937
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

22
00:02:07.437 --> 00:02:10.537
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
